# Makefile for course activities
# Override to use another Compose implementation, e.g. DC="podman compose"
DC ?= docker compose

# DC NAMES AN IMPLEMENTATION, AND NOTHING ELSE. STENCIL_COMPOSE below puts
# $(DC) FIRST and the pinned -f after it, so anything DC carries is prepended
# to the file list -- and a file named earlier is not overridden by the pin, it
# is MERGED WITH it. MEASURED against a real generated package:
#
#   $ DC='docker compose -f evil.yml' make -n format-md
#   docker compose -f evil.yml -f docker-compose.yml run --rm format-md
#
# and `config` on that pair keeps the pinned image -- so the service still
# LOOKS right -- while `privileged: true` and a `/:/hostfs` mount out of
# evil.yml land intact, because compose's merge only resolves keys that
# collide. That is the whole of stn-qli reopened through the sibling variable,
# so DC is checked rather than trusted.
#
# The check is "no word starting with a dash", not a list of known flags:
# `-f` and `--file` are the obvious smuggling routes, but `--project-directory`
# moves the base every relative volume source resolves against and
# `--env-file` re-points the variable file, and neither is a compose file by
# name. All four spellings pipeline.compose_command() falls through --
# "docker compose", "podman compose", "docker-compose", "podman-compose" --
# pass it, because none of them has a word beginning with a dash;
# "docker-compose" begins with a letter and is one word.
#
# LIKE THE COMPOSE_FILES GUARDS BELOW, THIS IS HONEST-MISTAKE PROTECTION AND
# NOT AN ATTACKER BOUNDARY. DC names the command make runs, so anyone who can
# set it can already run anything -- `DC='env COMPOSE_FILE=evil.yml docker
# compose'` carries no dash and passes this check. Its COMPOSE_FILE half is
# inert, measured: an explicit `-f` makes compose ignore COMPOSE_FILE
# entirely. The value is NOT inert overall, though, and an earlier version of
# this comment said it was -- the probe runtime below reads DC's FIRST word,
# so that value probes with `env`, which fails and silently turns the pull
# guard into an unconditional pull. See the note on prefix-command DC
# values there. What
# the check is for is the realistic case: a consumer who read "override DC to
# use another implementation", reached for the nearest variable to add a
# compose file too, and would otherwise get a silent merge rather than an
# error.
#
# THIS CHECK IS A PARSE-TIME SNAPSHOT OF DC, which is not the whole story --
# it reads DC as it stands HERE. A composition that includes this partial and
# then sets DC, and a DC defined in terms of another variable whose value
# arrives later -- a target-specific assignment, say -- both read as
# flag-free at this point and grow the flag when the recipe expands. `_stencil_pin_check` below asks the same question again at the point
# of use, where the value is the one the recipe will actually run with; this
# one stays because it fails earlier and more legibly for the ordinary case.
#
# ORIGIN IS DELIBERATELY NOT CHECKED HERE, unlike COMPOSE_FILES below.
# `export DC="podman compose"` is a legitimate thing to have in a shell profile
# on a podman-only machine, and the line above advertises the override without
# saying "command line only". Refusing it would break a working setup to
# prevent something this check already prevents directly.
ifneq ($(filter -%,$(DC)),)
$(error DC names a compose implementation only, e.g. DC="podman compose"; compose files belong in COMPOSE_FILES)
endif

# The compose file(s) every invocation below pins, so that a
# docker-compose.override.yml or a .env COMPOSE_FILE sitting in the package
# directory is never auto-discovered and merged by compose's own resolution
# (stn-qli). A consumer who genuinely wants such a file merged in still can --
# by naming it explicitly, e.g.
# `make doc COMPOSE_FILES="docker-compose.yml docker-compose.override.yml"`.
COMPOSE_FILES ?= docker-compose.yml

# BARE PATHS, NOT PRE-FLAGGED -- the `-f` is added below, at the point of use,
# by $(addprefix -f ,$(COMPOSE_FILES)), rather than being part of the value a
# consumer types. With the flag baked into the value, the obvious spelling --
# `make COMPOSE_FILES=docker-compose.yml`, the one a consumer will actually
# type -- would render as the literal `docker compose docker-compose.yml run
# ...` and fail confusingly, because there would be nothing left to add a
# `-f` in front of. Bare paths also keep the public interface to exactly "a
# list of compose files": anything else a consumer stuffs in here (`-p`,
# `--env-file`, `--profile`) would work once and then have to keep working.
#
# `?=` ALONE DOES NOT GUARD AGAINST AN EMPTY VALUE -- it only tests whether
# COMPOSE_FILES is DEFINED, not whether it is non-empty. MEASURED on a
# patched Makefile: `make COMPOSE_FILES=`, `COMPOSE_FILES= make`, and
# `MAKEFLAGS='COMPOSE_FILES=' make` all leave `?=` satisfied and still
# produce `docker compose  run --rm format-md` -- unpinned, working, and
# SILENT -- and that invocation really does let a planted override merge
# back in. So an empty value is refused outright below, rather than left to
# `?=` to catch, which it cannot.
ifeq ($(strip $(COMPOSE_FILES)),)
$(error COMPOSE_FILES must name at least one compose file, e.g. COMPOSE_FILES=docker-compose.yml)
endif

# THIS GUARD IS HONEST-MISTAKE PROTECTION, NOT AN ATTACKER BOUNDARY. An
# exported COMPOSE_FILES beats `?=` and silently drops the pin for every
# package built in that shell, with no output at all -- the realistic actor
# is a CI job that exported it once, legitimately, for something unrelated,
# and never revisited it; this turns that into a loud failure naming the
# variable. It is NOT a security boundary: `MAKEFLAGS='COMPOSE_FILES=evil.yml'
# make` reports COMPOSE_FILES's origin as "command line", not "environment",
# and slips straight past this check -- but anyone who can set your MAKEFLAGS
# already has the run of your environment, so this is not a boundary this
# guard is trying to hold.
ifeq ($(origin COMPOSE_FILES),environment)
$(error COMPOSE_FILES from the environment is ignored; pass it on the make command line)
endif

# STENCIL_COMPOSE, NOT COMPOSE. `COMPOSE` is short, unnamespaced, and exactly
# the name a consumer composition might pick for its own purposes; make's
# last-assignment-wins rule means such a definition would silently override
# this one and unpin every call site below, with no guard able to catch a
# wrong-but-non-empty value the way the two guards above catch an empty one.
# `COMPOSE_FILES` keeps the guessable, unprefixed name instead, because it is
# the one knob a consumer is actually meant to set.
#
# RECURSIVE '=', NOT ':='. With ':=' COMPOSE_FILES would be expanded once,
# right here, and a composition that includes this partial and then sets
# COMPOSE_FILES afterward -- from its own text, the command line, or the
# environment -- would have that later value silently ignored, while every
# other way of setting a make variable keeps working. That is the kind of
# mistake that hides because it looks like it works.
#
# THE THREE GUARDS ABOVE ARE PARSE-TIME SNAPSHOTS, AND THAT IS NOT ENOUGH ON
# ITS OWN. Each one sees only what was assigned ABOVE it, so a composition
# that includes this partial and then sets the variable -- which the
# paragraph directly above advertises as supported, and AGENTS.md documents
# as the way consumers compose these partials -- walks straight past all
# three. MEASURED, against a real generated package:
#
#   $ printf 'include base.mk\nCOMPOSE_FILES =\n' > Makefile && make -n format-md
#   docker compose  run --rm format-md          # unpinned, and SILENT
#
# which is exactly the mode the empty-value guard exists to kill. The same
# holds for DC, and DEFERRED EXPANSION defeats the flag check even without a
# late assignment -- a DC written in terms of a second variable, with that
# variable given a target-specific value of -f evil.yml, reads as flag-free
# at parse time and grows the flag when the recipe expands:
#
#   docker compose -f evil.yml -f docker-compose.yml run --rm format-md
#
# So the same two questions are asked again HERE, at the point of use, where
# the values are the ones the recipe will actually run with. This expands to
# nothing when both hold, so it costs a generated package nothing but the
# check. The parse-time guards stay: they fail earlier and more legibly for
# the ordinary case of a consumer typing `make COMPOSE_FILES=`, and a guard
# that fires at `make` startup beats one that fires midway through a build.
_stencil_pin_check = $(if $(strip $(COMPOSE_FILES)),,$(error COMPOSE_FILES must name at least one compose file -- e.g. COMPOSE_FILES=docker-compose.yml))$(if $(filter -%,$(DC)),$(error DC names a compose implementation only -- e.g. DC="podman compose" -- and compose files belong in COMPOSE_FILES))

STENCIL_COMPOSE = $(_stencil_pin_check)$(DC) $(addprefix -f ,$(COMPOSE_FILES))

# The runtime CLI that DC actually drives, DERIVED FROM DC AND ONLY FROM DC.
# Not a knob of its own: DC is the knob, advertised on line 2, and this
# follows it. The four spellings pipeline.compose_command() falls through are
# "docker compose", "podman compose", "docker-compose" and "podman-compose",
# and ensure_image below needs the bare binary name to run `image inspect`
# against. $(subst -, ,$(DC)) turns "docker-compose" into two words so
# $(firstword ...) yields "docker" the same way it already does for "docker
# compose"; the same holds for the two podman spellings. Do NOT simplify this
# to plain $(firstword $(DC)) -- that reads correctly for the two-word
# spellings but returns the whole standalone binary name ("docker-compose")
# for the other two, and neither standalone binary has an `image inspect`
# subcommand, so the probe below would fail to run at all on those hosts.
#
# WHY DERIVED RATHER THAN OVERRIDABLE (stn-3y8). This used to be spelled
# `CONTAINER ?=`, and `?=` means an environment value wins. Since ensure_image
# runs the result as a BARE COMMAND, an exported CONTAINER was executed by
# four targets, in the package directory, with both streams sent to
# /dev/null. MEASURED against a real generated package: with CONTAINER naming
# a planted script, `make format-md` ran it and exited 0 with nothing on
# either stream. The first attempt at that reproduction looked clean for
# exactly the reason this is dangerous -- the probe's output is discarded, so
# a marker printed to stdout proves nothing and the planted command had to
# write a file to be seen at all.
#
# It also silently disabled the pull guard below, which is the consequence a
# maintainer would not guess: the probe's EXIT STATUS is what decides whether
# `|| pull` runs. A planted probe exiting 0 means the pull never happens and
# a stale image is used with nothing said; one exiting non-zero means the
# pull happens on every build, forever.
#
# The name is namespaced for the reason STENCIL_COMPOSE is. CONTAINER is
# short, unnamespaced, and exactly the name a consumer composition might pick
# for its own "the name of my container"; COMPOSE_FILES keeps the guessable
# unprefixed name because it IS the knob a consumer is meant to set, and this
# is not one. A stray CONTAINER is now INERT rather than an error,
# deliberately: that name is generic enough that an unrelated export is
# plausible, and aborting every build over one would be worse than the bug it
# came from. The cost is that `make doc CONTAINER=podman` is silently
# ignored, since make does not warn about an unused command-line variable --
# recorded in STENCIL.md and the changelog rather than left to be discovered.
#
# WHY `override` AND NOT A PLAIN `=`. MEASURED, GNU Make 3.81 (macOS) and 4.3
# (CI); "loses" means the assignment here loses and the injected value wins:
#
#                     env    make -e   command line   MAKEFLAGS   GNUMAKEFLAGS
#   X ?= ...          loses  loses     loses          loses       loses (4.3)
#   X = ...           wins   loses     loses          loses       loses (4.3)
#   override X = ...  wins   wins      wins           wins        wins
#
# MAKEFLAGS is the route the COMPOSE_FILES origin guard above explicitly
# cannot close -- it reports origin "command line" and slips straight past.
# `override` closes it rather than inheriting that caveat. GNUMAKEFLAGS is a
# live injection route on 4.3 and inert on 3.81, so a green local run on
# macOS proves nothing about it and the test that covers it skips here and
# runs in CI. The assignment stays RECURSIVE, so a DC assigned later -- or
# built from a second variable, the deferred case _stencil_pin_check above
# exists for -- is still read where it is used.
#
# WHAT `override` DOES NOT CLOSE, stated because a guard that overclaims is
# worse than none. A TARGET-SPECIFIC or PATTERN-SPECIFIC assignment in the
# consuming makefile beats it -- `doc: STENCIL_CONTAINER = x` for that target,
# `%: STENCIL_CONTAINER = x` for every one -- while a GLOBAL plain assignment
# loses. MEASURED on 3.81 and 4.3, and MAKEFILES (an environment variable
# naming makefile text to read first) carries that form in from the
# environment. This is the same resolve-after-parse fact _stencil_pin_check
# above was written for. None of it is defensible in make, and none of it is
# an attacker route: every one needs the consuming makefile, which can
# already run anything.
#
# THE ESCAPE HATCH, and it has a real customer. A composition needing a
# differently-named runtime writes, after the include:
#
#   override STENCIL_CONTAINER = nerdctl
#
# An `override` is required; a plain assignment there loses to this one,
# which is the one sharp edge in the arrangement. Its customer is a DC that
# is not a bare implementation name: the derivation reads the FIRST word, so
# `sudo docker compose` probes with `sudo` (the standard rootful-Linux
# workaround), `env FOO=1 docker compose` with `env`, and
# `/opt/my-tools/docker compose` with `/opt/my` -- the subst splits the path
# too. All three pass the dash-flag guard above, and all three make the probe
# exit non-zero (MEASURED: 1, 127 and 127), which silently turns the pull
# guard into an unconditional pull. `podman-remote compose` is quieter and
# worse: it derives "podman" and probes the LOCAL image store while compose
# pulls to the remote. A consumer on any of those hosts sets the override.
override STENCIL_CONTAINER = $(firstword $(subst -, ,$(DC)))

# Pull an image only when it is not already present. Four targets want this and
# the two shells share no spelling of it, so it lives here rather than as four
# copies of the same ifeq: cmd, which is what make runs recipes through on
# Windows, has no grep.
#
# THE PROBE IS `image inspect`, NOT the old `-q`-flagged `images` listing.
# MEASURED: once an image reference carries a digest, running that old probe
# against `<ref>` prints nothing even though the image is present locally
# under that exact digest -- the bare-tag form of the same probe (against
# `<tag>` alone) prints the id, which is how this went unnoticed: the guard
# looked like it worked because some other tag happened to satisfy it, while
# compose went and pulled the pinned reference anyway on every build.
# `image inspect <ref>` checks the reference actually named, digest included,
# and reports the image absent when the digest is wrong -- which is what this
# guard always claimed to do and, before this fix, could not. The same
# measurement holds for podman, and is the reason STENCIL_CONTAINER exists
# above rather than the literal "docker" this used to say: on a podman-only host
# the old `-q`-flagged listing doesn't merely miss the digest, it fails
# outright, so the guard was an unconditional pull there regardless of this
# bug.
#
# One more thing MEASURED, not assumed: `image inspect` ignores a stale or
# bogus TAG once a digest is present -- `hello-world:BOGUSTAG@sha256:...`
# inspects successfully on both docker and podman. That is the same rule the
# registry itself applies (a digest resolves on its own; the tag alongside it
# is a human label), and it is this epic's whole argument for keying pins by
# digest rather than by tag -- so the probe is consistent with how pulling
# actually resolves the reference. But it means this guard does not and
# cannot validate that the tag half of a ref still describes the digest; it
# only proves the digest is present.
#
# Both branches pull through $(STENCIL_COMPOSE) rather than $(DC), so a
# missed-image pull is pinned the same as every other invocation. The
# Windows branch puts it inside a DOUBLE-QUOTED PowerShell -Command string,
# which is safe for COMPOSE_FILES's own default (no quotes or spaces in
# "docker-compose.yml") but is not generally safe: a COMPOSE_FILES entry
# containing a space would need its own quoting inside that string, which
# this does not attempt, so such a path breaks the Windows branch.
ifeq ($(OS),Windows_NT)
ensure_image = @powershell -NoProfile -Command "$(STENCIL_CONTAINER) image inspect '$(1)' *> $$null; if ($$LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { $(STENCIL_COMPOSE) pull $(2) }"
else
ensure_image = @$(STENCIL_CONTAINER) image inspect '$(1)' >/dev/null 2>&1 || $(STENCIL_COMPOSE) pull $(2)
endif

# Default target
ifeq ($(OS),Windows_NT)
help: ## Show this help message
	@echo Available targets:
	@powershell -NoProfile -Command "Get-Content $(MAKEFILE_LIST) | Select-String '^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]+:.*?## .*$$' | ForEach-Object { $$_ -match '^([a-zA-Z0-9_-]+):.*?## (.*)$$' | Out-Null; '  {0,-15} {1}' -f $$matches[1], $$matches[2] } | Select-Object -Unique"
else
help: ## Show this help message
	@echo "Available targets:"
	@grep -hE '^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]+:.*?## .*$$' $(MAKEFILE_LIST) | awk 'BEGIN {FS = ":.*?## "}; {printf "  %-15s %s\n", $$1, $$2}' | uniq
endif
